  "reading": "On C. elegans putative enhancers whose accessibility changes across development (J\u00e4nes et al. 2018, whole animals, six stages from embryo to young adult), each assigned to its stage of highest accessibility, with constraint measured on their exon-free bases (UCSC ce11 phyloP135way, stage 2's cut 2.08), the domain-rule gene is expressed at 5 coverage-TPM or more in long nuclear RNA and highest at the element's own stage for 1,631 of 3,938 elements in the top third by constrained fraction (41.4%), against 38.6% for the 4,094 in the bottom third at the same stage, position class, distance and measured length; difference +2.81 points, 95% -0.17 to +5.71 over 1-Mb clusters (within-stratum permutation p 0.00999; NOT_FALSIFIED). No difference was detected: the dose view seen beside stage 2 does not replicate on this source. This is not evidence that constrained elements lack function. The positive control separates: of 10,199 developmentally dynamic coding promoters, the nearest-TSS gene is stage-concordant for 3,936 (38.6%) against 22.3% at a matched distance (one-sided p 0.001). Secondary, stage 1's comparison on this source: on 13,852 elements the domain-rule gene is stage-concordant for 4,686 (33.8%) against 4,400 (31.8%) for the gene with the nearest 5' end; the rules differ on 2,919; difference +2.06 points, 95% +1.33 to +2.82 over 1-Mb clusters. On this endpoint the domain rule is ahead of nearest TSS, as in stage 1. The domain rule's genes match the element's stage more often than genes at a matched distance (19.3%, one-sided p 0.001). Conservation and accessibility are not function: this says the attributed gene is expressed when the element is open, not that the element acts on it, and it does not separate regulatory constraint from transcription-linked constraint. The RNA is from whole animals, so a stage concordance can come from any tissue.",
